data: {"level":1,"reviewed":{"level":1,"label":"concept","text":"Inverting a matrix means inverting its SVD, one singular value at a time. A zero singular value cannot be inverted, so it\nstays zero. A tiny one can be numerically zero, so the cutoff decides which singular values are trusted. The cutoff\nmust scale with the largest singular value, so that a matrix's overall size does not change its answer."},"hints_note":null}

